WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:04.373
A driving model can see the road, yet barely react when the scene changes.

2
00:00:04.373 --> 00:00:06.900
How do we make vision matter for planning?

3
00:00:07.300 --> 00:00:11.500
Insert a vehicle directly ahead, while keeping ego status and commands fixed.

4
00:00:11.500 --> 00:00:15.900
Existing models barely change their trajectories. This paper calls it blind planning.

5
00:00:16.300 --> 00:00:20.000
Many training examples are predictable from motion and route commands alone.

6
00:00:20.000 --> 00:00:23.900
Direct trajectory supervision can reward that shortcut, leaving visual evidence underused.

7
00:00:24.300 --> 00:00:29.770
The key idea is inspired by inverse kinematics: anchor an action to a start and a target.

8
00:00:29.770 --> 00:00:31.900
Here, both anchors are visual states.

9
00:00:32.300 --> 00:00:36.130
Three cameras feed a vision encoder and a compact language model.

10
00:00:36.130 --> 00:00:41.900
The model uses motion and route context to predict future visual tokens, rather than future pixels.

11
00:00:42.300 --> 00:00:47.564
A separate diffusion network turns the current and predicted visual states into a trajectory.

12
00:00:47.564 --> 00:00:51.900
Ego status and commands have no direct path into this decoder.

13
00:00:52.300 --> 00:00:55.517
Future-state supervision trains the visual representation, alongside scene question answering.

14
00:00:55.517 --> 00:00:59.900
Trajectory gradients stop at the conditioning tokens, separating visual learning from diffusion learning.

15
00:01:00.300 --> 00:01:03.664
Obstacle insertion shortens the average 3-second endpoint by 1.04 m.

16
00:01:03.664 --> 00:01:07.900
Ours decelerates on 63.8% of perturbed samples, compared with 44.2% for Baseline.

17
00:01:08.300 --> 00:01:13.593
The appendix inserts the same vehicle at five placements, varying position and size.

18
00:01:13.593 --> 00:01:19.900
In this example, Near and Far shorten our trajectory much more than the sky insertions.

19
00:01:20.300 --> 00:01:25.816
Far and SkyFar use the same sprite scale of 0.5. Only the insertion position changes.

20
00:01:25.816 --> 00:01:31.900
With the same noise seeds, Ours responds more strongly to the road placement in this example.

21
00:01:32.300 --> 00:01:39.341
At the same sprite scale, mean absolute endpoint change is 1.81 m on the road versus 1.04 m in the sky.

22
00:01:39.341 --> 00:01:43.900
Far produces a larger absolute response than SkyFar in 65.5% of paired frames.

23
00:01:44.300 --> 00:01:49.000
Across this left turn, our predicted path follows the green ground-truth trajectory more closely.

24
00:01:49.000 --> 00:01:53.900
Selected clip mean ADE: Baseline 6.00 m → Ours 0.30 m. Lower is better.

25
00:01:54.300 --> 00:01:59.162
At this intersection, our prediction follows the right turn while Baseline diverges from the reference.

26
00:01:59.162 --> 00:02:03.900
Selected clip mean ADE: Baseline 3.02 m → Ours 0.15 m. Lower is better.

27
00:02:04.300 --> 00:02:09.162
In this urban sequence, our predicted trajectory better matches the reference path and travel distance.

28
00:02:09.162 --> 00:02:13.900
Selected clip mean ADE: Baseline 2.34 m → Ours 0.10 m. Lower is better.

29
00:02:14.300 --> 00:02:19.000
In this nighttime sequence, our predicted path stays closer to the green ground-truth trajectory.

30
00:02:19.000 --> 00:02:23.900
Selected clip mean ADE: Baseline 2.40 m → Ours 0.18 m. Lower is better.

31
00:02:24.300 --> 00:02:30.124
A 0.5B LLM backbone reaches 92.2 PDMS on NAVSIM-v1 and 90.6 EPDMS on NAVSIM-v2.

32
00:02:30.124 --> 00:02:34.900
Gains over the matched Baseline: +19.0 PDMS and +20.4 EPDMS points.

33
00:02:35.300 --> 00:02:37.321
Grounding Driving VLA via Inverse Kinematics.

34
00:02:37.321 --> 00:02:41.900
A visual-state interface for more responsive planning, using cameras and a compact backbone.
